{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Every meal-offering that you bring to YHWH shall not be made with leaven.</b> He prohibited bringing the meal-offering leavened, and afterward said, \"You shall not burn from it as incense,\" to prohibit that one not leaven the fistful and burn it as incense, and it would enter the category of \"you shall not burn from it as a fire-offering.\" Every term of הקטרה (burning as incense) is an expression of קטרת הסמים (incense of spices), for the perfumers say that honey was excellent for the incense, but the Torah forbade it (Torat Kohanim, parashah 12:4). And He said \"from it\" to prohibit part of it, that is, half a fistful, and likewise one incurs lashes for mixing it in, from the amplification of the verse which says, \"for all leaven and all honey,\" as is explained in Menahot (56a) and in Pesahim (43a). And the reason the verse says \"מנחה היא\" (it is a meal-offering) (verses 6, 15) and does not write \"הוא\" (he) — and likewise everywhere the feminine form is used in the masculine's stead — is explained in the passage \"הנה אנכי שולח מלאך\" (Exodus 23:20), and likewise \"האשה ההוא\" (Numbers 5:31; Deuteronomy 17:5), because the feminine is included within the masculine in potential. And the reason for leaven and honey may perhaps be as the Rav says in the Guide for the Perplexed (III:46): he said that he found in their books that it was the practice of idolaters to offer all their meal-offerings leavened and to mix honey into all their sacrifices, and therefore He forbade them for the altar. And our Sages said a similar thing (Sifrei, Shofetim 146) concerning the massebah: that it was chosen in the days of the patriarchs, but afterward HaShem hated it because they made it a statute for idolatry, as He said, \"which HaShem your Elohim hates\" (Deuteronomy 16:22). And regarding the reason for salt, he said as well that it is because they would despise it and not offer it at all. And it is also possible that since it is not a path of honor for the bread of HaShem to be unsalted and bland without salt, as in the reason of \"offer it now to your governor\" (Malachi 1:8), and therefore wood and blood, which do not require salt, are minimal (Menahot 20a) — or perhaps there is in all these a hidden reason concealed from us. And the verse spoke concerning leaven and honey in plural language, for He is speaking with Aaron and his sons, and then returned and said, \"And every meal-offering of yours you shall season with salt,\" for He turns back to the one bringing the meal-offering, about whom He spoke at the beginning of the passage (Leviticus 2:7), \"and if the meal-offering of a pan is your offering.\" And the reason is that salting is valid through a non-priest, like pouring and kneading. And the meaning of \"upon every offering of yours\" is upon all your offerings, for all offerings require salt just like the meal-offering:"], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "In the phrase ומעטו העצים והדם, I read מעטו as \"they are minimal/infrequent,\" though the syntax is compressed and the reference to Menahot 20a (which lists items exempt from salt) may suggest \"they are few\" in number."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:50:05.749446811Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}